---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Types of Lead-Acid Batteries: The Complete Family';
const description = 'The lead-acid family — flooded, EFB, AGM, gel, SLI and deep-cycle — organised by electrolyte form and plate design. How the six types differ and which fits which job.';
const canonical = '/lead-acid/types/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'Types of lead-acid batteries',
  articleSection: 'Lead-Acid Battery Knowledge',
  dateModified: '2026-09-16',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What are the main types of lead-acid batteries?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Organised by electrolyte: flooded (liquid), EFB (enhanced flooded with carbon), AGM (absorbed glass mat) and gel (silica-gelled) — and by duty: SLI starting vs deep-cycle. The six common labels all map onto these two dimensions.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Which lead-acid type should I choose?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Follow the duty: basic cars take flooded or EFB, start-stop cars require EFB or AGM, deep-cycling jobs (solar, marine house) take AGM, gel or deep-cycle flooded. The vehicle or application specification usually decides it for you.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-16"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Fundamentals · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>Types of Lead-Acid Batteries: The Complete Family</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The lead-acid family has many labels — SLI, EFB, AGM, gel, deep-cycle — and they all reduce to two
        questions: how is the electrolyte held, and what is the battery asked to do?
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> The types organise by <strong>electrolyte form</strong> —
        <strong>flooded</strong> (liquid), <strong>EFB</strong> (enhanced flooded with carbon),
        <strong>AGM</strong> (glass-mat sealed), <strong>gel</strong> (silica-gelled) — and by
        <strong>duty</strong>: <strong>SLI</strong> starting vs <strong>deep-cycle</strong>. Every common label
        maps onto those two dimensions.
      </div>

      <h2>What are the main types of lead-acid batteries?</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Electrolyte</th><th>Best at</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Flooded SLI</strong></td><td>Free liquid</td><td>Cheap cranking</td><td>Basic cars</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>EFB</strong></td><td>Liquid + carbon</td><td>Cycling at low cost</td><td>Start-stop (entry)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>AGM</strong></td><td>Glass mat, sealed</td><td>Power + cycling, sealed</td><td>Start-stop, premium, UPS</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Gel</strong></td><td>Silica gel, sealed</td><td>Deep cycling, any position</td><td>Solar, mobility</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Deep-cycle flooded</strong></td><td>Free liquid, thick plates</td><td>Deep discharge endurance</td><td>Marine, forklift, off-grid</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>What decides which type fits?</h2>
      <p>
        The electrolyte form sets <strong>maintenance and safety</strong> (sealed vs watering); the plate design
        sets <strong>duty</strong> (thin plates crank, thick plates cycle — see
        <a href="/lead-acid/deep-cycle-vs-starting/">starting vs deep-cycle</a>). A type is the combination: AGM
        deep-cycle, for example, is a sealed design with cycling-oriented plates. Reading any label through the two
        dimensions explains what it is for.
      </p>

      <h2>Which type should I choose?</h2>
      <p>
        Follow the duty, then the price: start-stop cars <em>require</em> EFB or AGM
        (see <a href="/lead-acid/efb-battery/">EFB</a> and <a href="/lead-acid/agm-battery/">AGM</a>); basic cars
        take flooded; deep-cycling jobs take AGM, gel or deep-cycle flooded
        (see <a href="/lead-acid/agm-vs-gel/">AGM vs gel</a>). The specification usually decides — and when it does
        not, the duty does.
      </p>

      <h2>Our Interpretation</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My read:</strong> The lead-acid family looks complicated until you see the two dimensions behind
          every label — electrolyte form and duty — after which every type in the catalogue is just a cell in a
          2×2 table, and the purchase rule is simply matching the cell to the job.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>The reasoning:</strong> Labels multiply; dimensions do not. Once the map is known — sealed or not,
          cranking or cycling — the buyer stops being confused by marketing names and reads each battery for what
          it is: a specific answer to a specific duty.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What are the main types of lead-acid batteries?</h3>
      <p>Organised by electrolyte: flooded (liquid), EFB (enhanced flooded with carbon), AGM (absorbed glass mat) and gel (silica-gelled) — and by duty: SLI starting vs deep-cycle. The six common labels all map onto these two dimensions.</p>
      <h3>Which lead-acid type should I choose?</h3>
      <p>Follow the duty: basic cars take flooded or EFB, start-stop cars require EFB or AGM, deep-cycling jobs (solar, marine house) take AGM, gel or deep-cycle flooded. The vehicle or application specification usually decides it for you.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from battery engineering references and BCI documentation.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery Council International (BCI) — battery type classifications.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer documentation — type construction and duty guidance.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
